<exercise label="counting-harder-coffee-order">
<title>Coffee Order</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
     	A coffee shop has 4 different types of coffee. You can order your coffee in a small, medium, or large cup. You can also choose whether you want to add cream, sugar, or milk (any combination is possible, for example, you can choose to add all three). In how many ways can you order your coffee?
    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>


<exercise label="counting-harder-housing-multinomial-prob">

<title>Campus Housing Room Assignments</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Refer to the problem above with the same name. A group of <m>12</m> college students are selected to live on a specialized floor in a residence hall. The floor has exactly <m>6</m> rooms available that must be filled: one triple room (<m>3</m> students), three double rooms (<m>2</m> students each), and three single rooms (<m>1</m> student each). 
        </p>
        <p>
            
                Suppose two of the students, Alex and Jordan, are best friends. What is the probability that they are assigned to room together in the same room? Recall: The housing department assigns the <m>12</m> students completely at random to fill these specific spots.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answers>
            <answer>
                <string>6/11</string>
            </answer>
        </answers>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Alex and Jordan cannot room together in any of the single rooms. Calculate the number of favorable assignments if they are placed together in the triple room, and add that to the configurations where they are placed together in one of the three double rooms. Then, divide by the total possible ways (<m>1,663,200</m>).
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find the probability, we look at the two distinct scenarios where Alex and Jordan share a room:
            </p>
            <p>
                1. <term>Case 1: They share the Triple Room.</term> Two of the three spots are locked down by Alex and Jordan. The remaining <m>10</m> students must be distributed into the 1 leftover spot in the triple, the three double rooms, and the three single rooms. This yields:
                <me>\frac{10!}{1! \times 2! \times 2! \times 2! \times 1! \times 1! \times 1!} = 453,600 \text{ ways}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                2. <term>Case 2: They share a Double Room.</term> There are 3 distinct double rooms they could share. If they take over one specific double room, it becomes completely filled. The remaining <m>10</m> students must fill the triple room, the other 2 double rooms, and the three single rooms:
                <me>\frac{10!}{3! \times 2! \times 2! \times 1! \times 1! \times 1!} = 151,200 \text{ ways per room}</me>
                Multiplying by the <m>3</m> optional double rooms gives: <m>3 \times 151,200 = 453,600</m> ways.
            </p>
            <p>
                3. <term>Combine and Calculate Probability:</term> Adding the favorable layouts gives <m>453,600 + 453,600 = 907,200</m> successful configurations. Dividing by our total from Part 1:
                <me>P(\text{Rooming Together}) = \frac{907,200}{1,663,200} = \frac{6}{11} \approx 0.5455</me>
            </p>
        </solution>

       
</exercise>

 <exercise label="counting-harder-powerball-lose">

<introduction>
  <p>
    See the Powerball in-class activity. 
    
    
  </p>
</introduction>

<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What outcomes result in someone not winning a prize? 
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>

<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Compute the probability for each of these outcomes.
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>

<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the overall probability of not winning a prize when playing Powerball? 
    </p>
  </statement>
</task>

</exercise>
